  "notes": "きっぱり expresses doing something in a clean, decisive manner without hesitation or ambiguity. It conveys firm resolve, especially when refusing, quitting, or stating a position.\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- きっぱり{断|ことわ}る: to flatly refuse\n- きっぱりやめる: to quit for good\n- きっぱりと{言|い}う: to say clearly and firmly\n- きっぱり{別|わか}れる: to make a clean break\n\nGRAMMAR:\nOften followed by と (きっぱりと) before verbs for emphasis. Can also be used with する: きっぱりした{態度|たいど} (a firm attitude).\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- はっきり: clearly (focuses on clarity of expression rather than decisiveness)\n- すっぱり: cleanly, completely (emphasizes completeness of a cut or break)\n- きちんと: properly, neatly (focuses on correctness rather than resolve)",
